\description{
Splits the change of the event rate between a base and a comparison
sample into the part due to the mix (the population moved across the
score bands) and the part due to the rates (the bands themselves have a
different event rate), on bands frozen on the base. With \code{by}, every
period or segment is compared with the base.
}
\section{Decomposition}{


With \eqn{p_k} the share of band \eqn{k} among the rows with a known
outcome and \eqn{r_k} its event rate, on the base (\eqn{b}) and on the
comparison (\eqn{c}):
\deqn{mix_k = (p_{c,k} - p_{b,k}) (r_{b,k} + r_{c,k}) / 2, \qquad
      rate_k = (r_{c,k} - r_{b,k}) (p_{b,k} + p_{c,k}) / 2.}
The weights are the midpoints of the two samples, so the effects carry no
interaction term and add up exactly:
\eqn{\sum_k mix_k + \sum_k rate_k = R_c - R_b}, the change of the overall
rate. A band empty in one sample has no rate there; it takes the rate of
the other sample, so its rate effect is 0 and its whole contribution is
a mix effect (the columns \code{rate_base} and \code{rate_cmp} keep the missing
rate as \code{NA}). \code{total} is \code{mix_effect + rate_effect}.

The summary adds, per comparison, \code{share_mix = mix_total / delta} (\code{NA}
when the rate did not change; it can fall outside [0, 1] when the two
effects have opposite signs) and the PSI of the band shares against the
base with its n-adjusted critical value at \code{1 - level} (see \code{\link[=scr_psi]{scr_psi()}}).
}

\section{Tests}{


\code{p_rate} is the two-sided p-value of the change of the rate of the band,
on the unweighted counts: Fisher's exact test when the smallest expected
count of the two-by-two table (sample by outcome) is below 5, and the
pooled two-proportion z test otherwise. \code{p_rate_adj} is Holm-adjusted
across the bands of the comparison. A band empty in either sample has no
test.
}

\section{Input}{


\describe{
\item{A score study}{An object from \code{\link[=scr_bands]{scr_bands()}} or \code{\link[=scr_tiers]{scr_tiers()}} with
at least two samples: its cuts and labels are used, \code{base} is one of
its samples (default its reference) and \code{compare} the others.}
\item{A scorecard}{\code{base} and \code{compare} are scored samples (\code{"train"}
against \code{"holdout"}). With \code{by}, a column of the scored samples such
as \code{"date"}, the rows of both samples are pooled and grouped by that
column; \code{base} is then one of its values (default the first) and
\code{compare} the others.}
\item{A data.frame}{\code{by} names the column that tells the groups apart (a
period, a segment or a sample label); \code{base} is one of its values
(default the first level) and \code{compare} the others.}
}
The values of \code{by} are read in the order of the levels of a factor, in
numeric order for a numeric column and as sorted text otherwise (dates
included); the first is the default base. Rows with a missing \code{by} value
are left out. Rows with a missing outcome are left out of the shares and
of the rates, so that the effects add up to the change of the rate.
}

